_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 29
---
order: 29
---
short_label: III.29
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DEF|DEFt} be equal circles, and in them let equal circumferences {BGC} and {EHF} be cut off.  Join the straight lines {BC} and {EF}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="400" height="220" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.29 diagram" src="propIII29.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's page reuses the III.28 applet here unchanged — title "III.28",
    // image propIII28.gif, and III.28's lettering: the triangles are ABK and
    // DEL on the chords AB and DE, with G and H under them. III.29's prose
    // is lettered differently: the chords are BC and EF, the circumferences
    // BGC and EHF, the triangles BKC and ELF. On his figure "BC" is the
    // chord from the bottom-right slider up to the top point and "BGC" the
    // greater arc round through G and A, so no walk of his sentences fits
    // his figure.
    //
    // The figure below is his figure RE-LETTERED to his prose, and nothing
    // else: the same fourteen elements with the same coordinates, only the
    // letters permuted on each circle (A→B, B→C, C→A; D→E, E→F, F→D) so that
    // the drawn triangle is BKC on the chord BC over the arc BGC, and ELF on
    // EF over EHF. Pixel for pixel it is his drawing with different labels.
    // This is the one deliberate departure from "the resting figure stays
    // as Joyce drew it" in this deck; revert the element list to III.28's
    // and the deck cannot be authored. The lettering is Heath's (Green Lion
    // Press): C→A, A→B, B→C; F→D, D→E, E→F relative to III.28's figure. The
    // noscript image is a render of this figure, not his propIII28.gif.
    //
    // Additions, as in III.28: transparent twins of the two circles (they
    // carry the opaque near-white default face, so are never highlighted —
    // #140), real black chords coincident with the triangles' bases so the
    // chords can be joined before the centres are taken, transparent radii,
    // transparent arcs for the two circumferences, and one marker per
    // centre angle. Arcs draw counter-clockwise on screen from first arm to
    // last, so B,G,C is the lesser arc through G; both sweep forward (a
    // lesser arc always does — #172 bites only the greater one).
    var GIVEN = ["ABC","DEF","A","B","C","D","E","F"];
    var ARCS  = GIVEN.concat(["G","H","BGC","EHF"]);
    var CHORD = ARCS.concat(["BC","EF"]);
    var CENT  = CHORD.concat(["K","L"]);
    var JOIN  = CENT.concat(["BCK","EFL","BK","KC","EL","LF"]);
    var ANG   = JOIN.concat(["angBKC","angELF"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.29",
        elements: [
            "K;point;free;110,110",
            "A;point;free;110,30;black",
            "ABC;circle;radius;K,A",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABC,60,170",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABC,160,170",
            "L;point;free;290,110",
            "DEF;circle;radius;L,K,A",
            "E;point;circleSlider;DEF,240,170",
            "D;point;circleSlider;DEF,290,30",
            "F;point;similar;L,E,K,B,C",
            "G;point;circleSlider;ABC,110,190",
            "H;point;circleSlider;DEF,290,190",
            "BCK;polygon;triangle;B,C,K;0;0;black;random",
            "EFL;polygon;triangle;E,F,L;0;0;black;random",
            // Transparent twins of the two circles — the highlight targets.
            "ABCt;circle;radius;K,A;0;0;0;0",
            "DEFt;circle;radius;L,K,A;0;0;0;0",
            // The chords, coincident with the triangles' bases.
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            // The radii the proof names — the triangles already draw them.
            "BK;line;connect;B,K;0;0;0",
            "KC;line;connect;K,C;0;0;0",
            "EL;line;connect;E,L;0;0;0",
            "LF;line;connect;L,F;0;0;0",
            // The two circumferences cut off.
            "BGC;sector;arc;B,G,C;0;0;0;0",
            "EHF;sector;arc;E,H,F;0;0;0;0",
            // The angles at the centres — equal, so one colour for both.
            "angBKC;sector;angleMarker;K,B,C;0;0;3b6ea5;9ec5e8",
            "angELF;sector;angleMarker;L,E,F;0;0;3b6ea5;9ec5e8"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle): K, A, L free; B, C, E, D,
        // G, H circleSliders. F is derived (`similar`). The centres are not
        // taken until slide 5 and G, H arrive with the circumferences on
        // slide 2, so those four are deferred; the rest are the circles'
        // own letters, introduced on slide 1.
        deferDraggables: ["K","L","G","H"],
        aliases: { "CB": "BC", "FE": "EF", "KB": "BK", "CK": "KC",
                   "LE": "EL", "FL": "LF", "CGB": "BGC", "FHE": "EHF",
                   "BKC": "angBKC", "CKB": "angBKC",
                   "ELF": "angELF", "FLE": "angELF" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”).
            { text: "In equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.29" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DEF|DEFt} be equal circles,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["ABCt","DEFt"] },
            { text: "and in them let equal circumferences {BGC} and {EHF} be cut off.",
              visible: ARCS,
              highlighted: ["BGC", "EHF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BGC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "EHF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Join the straight lines {BC} and {EF}.",
              visible: CHORD,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "EF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "I say that {BC} equals {EF}.",
              visible: CHORD,
              highlighted: ["BC","EF"] },
            { text: "Take the centers {K} and {L} of the circles.",
              visible: CENT,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "K", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "L", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the centers K and L", ref: "III.1" }] },
            { text: "Join {BK}, {KC}, {EL}, and {LF}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["BCK", "EFL"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BCK", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "EFL", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Now, since the circumference {BGC} equals the circumference {EHF}, the angle {BKC} also equals the angle {ELF}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BGC", "EHF", "angBKC", "angELF"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angBKC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angELF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "angles at the centers on equal circumferences", ref: "III.27" }] },
            { text: "And, since the circles {ABC|ABCt} and {DEF|DEFt} are equal, the radii are also equal, therefore the two sides {BK} and {KC} equal the two sides {EL} and {LF},",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BK","KC","EL","LF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equal circles have equal radii", ref: "III.Def.1" }] },
            { text: "and they contain equal angles, therefore the base {BC} equals the base {EF}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angBKC","angELF","BC","EF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "two sides and the angle between them", ref: "I.4" }] },
            { text: "Therefore in equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["BC","EF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.29") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.4":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI4/",
        "III.Def.1": "/elements/books/bookIII/definitions/defIII1/",
        "III.1":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.27":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII27/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that {BC} equals {EF}.

Take the centers {K} and {L} of the circles.  Join {BK}, {KC}, {EL}, and {LF}. [!just III.1]

Now, since the circumference {BGC} equals the circumference {EHF}, the angle {BKC} also equals the angle {ELF}. [!just III.27]

And, since the circles {ABC|ABCt} and {DEF|DEFt} are equal, the radii are also equal, therefore the two sides {BK} and {KC} equal the two sides {EL} and {LF}, and they contain equal angles, therefore the base {BC} equals the base {EF}. [!just I.4]

Therefore *in equal circles straight lines that cut off equal circumferences are equal.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
